*.rej

# ==============================================================================
# Docker
# ==============================================================================
docker-compose.override.yml
test/.secrets

# Editor and tooling scratch files.  The settings writer leaves these behind
# and one of them got committed once.
*.tmp.*

# Testing scratch.  The old docker rig lived here and was never meant to ship,
# so it was taken out of the history as well, not just the current tree.  This
# line keeps a scratch test/ folder from getting committed by accident.
#
# Neither test/ nor checks/ is committed.  Both are internal testing rather
# than part of the product, and they stay on the machines that run them.
test/
checks/

# Everything under tools/ stays on disk and out of git, at the owner's
# direction, and so does checks/ above for the same reason.  It is scaffolding rather than part of the product: the machinery
# that builds the user guide, the applying test pass, the mutation harness and
# the bill of materials generator.  What they produce is what ships, and that
# is committed: the guide in docs/, the bill of materials in sbom/, and the
# test plan handed over as a document.
#
# Read this before adding a file under tools/ and expecting it to be there
# after a clone.  Nothing tells you it was left out; being ignored is exactly
# what this line asks for, which is the same trap the test/ line above warns
# about one directory up.
tools/

# The list of names that must never be committed.  Kept out of git itself,
# because writing private identifiers into the repository in order to keep them
# out of it defeats the point.
.githooks/forbidden.txt

# The applying test pass keeps its evidence on disk too: screenshots, the json
# it records as it goes, the browser session and the document it builds.  All
# of it is covered by the tools/ line above; these are kept as a note of what
# is down there, because it is tens of megabytes and it is regenerated by
# running the suites.
